HTTP -> HTTP stands for hyper text transfer protocol. It is used for the communication between client and web server.
HTTP is not the only way to communicate between a client and a web server.
HTTP is just the most common and standard way, but there are several other protocols depending on the use case.

🧠 1. HTTP / HTTPS (Most common)
Used in almost every web app
Request → Response model
Stateless
Example: REST APIs, websites

👉 You already know this one.

⚡ 2. WebSockets
Full-duplex (both client & server can send data anytime)
Persistent connection (doesn’t close after one request)
Great for:
Chat apps
Live notifications
Real-time games
🚀 3. gRPC
High-performance communication (uses HTTP/2 internally)
Uses Protocol Buffers instead of JSON
Common in:
Microservices
Internal backend communication
📡 4. Server-Sent Events (SSE)
Server can push updates to client
Client cannot send back on same connection
Simpler than WebSockets
📨 5. Message Queues / Brokers
Not direct request-response
Uses systems like:
Kafka
RabbitMQ
Async communication
Used in large scalable systems
🔌 6. Raw TCP / UDP (Low level)
Direct socket programming
Used in:
Games
Streaming
Custom protocols
🧩 So what's the real picture?
For normal apps → HTTP is enough
For real-time → WebSockets
For high-performance services → gRPC
For scalable systems → Message queues
🌐 What is HTTP Request & Response?

👉 HTTP works on a simple model:

Client sends Request
Server sends Response

Example:

You open a website → browser sends request
Server sends back HTML → browser renders it
📩 1. HTTP Request (Client → Server)

An HTTP request has 4 main components:

1. Request Line

Contains:

Method (what action you want)
URL (where)
HTTP version

Example:

GET /users HTTP/1.1

👉 Common methods:

GET → fetch data
POST → send data
PUT → update
DELETE → remove
2. Headers

Extra information about the request

Example:

Host: example.com
User-Agent: Chrome
Authorization: Bearer token123
Content-Type: application/json

👉 Used for:

Authentication
Content type
Caching
Cookies
3. Body (Optional)

Data sent to server (mostly in POST/PUT)

Example:

{
  "name": "Piyush",
  "age": 22
}
4. Query Parameters (Part of URL)

Extra data in URL

Example:

/users?id=10&sort=asc
📤 2. HTTP Response (Server → Client)

Also has 4 main components:

1. Status Line

Contains:

HTTP version
Status code
Status message

Example:

HTTP/1.1 200 OK
2. Status Codes (Very Important)

👉 Categories:

2xx → Success
3xx → Redirection
4xx → Client error
5xx → Server error

Examples:

200 → OK
201 → Created
400 → Bad Request
401 → Unauthorized
404 → Not Found
500 → Internal Server Error
3. Headers

Same concept as request

Example:

Content-Type: application/json
Set-Cookie: sessionId=abc123
Cache-Control: no-cache
4. Body

Actual data returned by server

Example:

{
  "message": "User fetched successfully",
  "data": {
    "name": "Piyush"
  }
}
🔁 Full Flow Example
Request:
POST /login HTTP/1.1
Host: example.com
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "test@gmail.com",
  "password": "1234"
}
Response:
HTTP/1.1 200 OK
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "token": "xyz123"
}
🧠 Key Concepts You MUST remember
HTTP is stateless → every request is independent
Headers carry metadata
Body carries actual data
Status codes tell result of request
🚀 Interview Tip

If interviewer asks:
👉 “Explain HTTP request/response”

You can say:

“HTTP is a client-server protocol where the client sends a request containing method, headers, and optional body, and the server responds with a status code, headers, and body.”